+/*
+ * The hybrid interrupt table, and the moment the kernel takes ownership of it.
+ *
+ * Called once, at the end of trap_init(), when everything a fault needs is in
+ * place: idt_table holds the kernel's real handlers (idt_setup_traps), the TSS
+ * descriptor is written and the IST stacks exist (cpu_init_exception_handling),
+ * and the syscall MSRs are set (cpu_init). Until here the guest ran on the
+ * IDT the host built, where every vector is a stub that reports and crosses
+ * back; that is why the ring-3 milestone ended on a *reported* page fault --
+ * demand paging cannot happen while the host owns vector 14.
+ *
+ * The table this loads is a hybrid, because the machine is shared:
+ *
+ *   0-31 except NMI    the kernel's own handlers, so exceptions -- above all
+ *                      the page fault -- are serviced, not reported.
+ *   NMI and 32-255     the host's stubs, copied out of the IDT that is live
+ *                      right now (sidt), because external interrupts are
+ *                      Windows' clock and IPIs and must keep crossing back.
+ *                      This also covers 0x80: IA32_EMULATION put int80 there,
+ *                      but on this machine 0x80 is a hardware vector.
