+ * The stub gates are rebuilt rather than copied verbatim: the host's carry
+ * IST1, which in the kernel's TSS is the double-fault stack. With IST 0 an
+ * interrupt from ring 0 stays on the interrupted stack and one from ring 3
+ * takes RSP0, exactly like any other kernel entry.
+ *
+ * Ordering within the flip is what makes it interrupt-safe. Real IF is on and
+ * cannot be masked, so every intermediate state must be consistent: gate
+ * copies into idt_table change nothing until lidt; the GDT swap is transparent
+ * because the host's table was built to the kernel's exact layout, selector
+ * for selector; ltr indexes GDT_ENTRY_TSS in whichever of the two is live and
+ * both hold a valid TSS by now. An interrupt landing between any two of these
+ * instructions is delivered through a coherent pair of tables. The direct
+ * (writable) GDT is loaded, never the read-only fixmap alias, because the
+ * world switch clears the TSS busy bit in the live GDT before every ltr --
+ * through a read-only mapping that write is a page fault inside the crossing.
+ *
+ * After this, GDTR/IDTR/TR ride the world switch like every other register:
+ * saved live on each exit, restored on each entry.
