+/*
+ * The virtual interrupt flag is saved and restored across an exception, the
+ * way the hardware flag is by the gate and the iretq.
+ *
+ * A cooperative guest runs with the real IF permanently on and substitutes a
+ * variable for it (asm/irqflags.h in this tree). That variable has the
+ * lifecycle problem the hardware flag solves in silicon: an interrupt gate
+ * clears IF on delivery and iretq restores it, so a handler's
+ * cond_local_irq_enable()/cond_local_irq_disable() pair nets out to nothing.
+ * With a virtual flag and neither the gate nor the iretq touching it, every
+ * exception the kernel serviced left virtual interrupts off permanently --
+ * which announced itself as "initcall msr_init returned with disabled
+ * interrupts", once per MSR-probing initcall, on the first boot after the
+ * kernel took ownership of its IDT and began fixing up its own rdmsr_safe
+ * faults.
+ *
