+ * every exception passes. It is deliberately NOT stashed in regs->flags,
+ * although that is where the hardware keeps its equivalent and the first
+ * version of this did exactly that. The real IF bit in a frame is not
+ * bookkeeping: it is what the CPU loads on the way out, and the host is a
+ * live Windows kernel that dies if its core stops taking interrupts. Frames
+ * outlive handlers -- ret_from_fork gives a child its parent's saved pt_regs
+ * -- so a cleared IF bit escapes into a context that never runs this exit
+ * path, and the guest resumes with real interrupts off. The machine freezes
+ * hard enough to need the power button, which is what it did.
